# call-in-dead-position.tflw — CHECK-ONLY FIXTURE (M87): a call written where its value is never
# computed — demonstrates `tflw check`'s teaching diagnostic (TF040: checker, `checkCalls`, tflw
# M87).
#
# This one was not on the review's list. It was found while fixing `A4-03`, by probing what the
# checker's blind spot to callees was actually hiding, and it is the worst-behaved of the four:
#
#   api POST /tickets body { subject: make subject() }   →  sends {}          and reports ✓
#   api POST /tickets body { tags: [make subject()] }    →  sends [null]      and reports ✓
#   give make subject()                                  →  returns nothing   and reports ✓
#
# A green suite asserting against a request nobody wrote. tflw evaluates a call in exactly two
# places — a bare call step, and the *whole* right-hand side of a `let` — because running one is
# asynchronous while every other value is computed synchronously. Anywhere else it parses, checks,
# and silently yields nothing.
#
# Reported alone: this file's call is also unknown *and* wrong-arity, and tflw says neither, because
# the position is what has to change first and the rest may go with it.
#
# Run explicitly (dot-directory, invalid syntax — see bad-keyword.tflw's header for why):
#   npx tflw check tests/.checkonly/call-in-dead-position.tflw

action make subject()
  give "Printer jam"

test "interpolates a call straight into a request body"
  api POST /tickets body { subject: make subject(), body: "It jammed again." }
  expect status equals 201
